---
# MONGOD, ITS REPLICA SET, AND ITS BACKUPS.
#
# WHAT WATCHES MONGOD HERE, AND FROM WHERE. Two vantage points, and the file is in two groups
# because they fail independently:
#
#   `mongodb`           node_exporter's systemd collector plus the textfile the backup timer
#                       writes. Outside the process: is the unit running, did the dump run.
#   `mongodb-exporter`  mongodb_exporter on mongo-1 (infra/nix/modules/roles/mongodb-exporter.nix),
#                       authenticated as `kinowo_monitor` and asking mongod itself. Inside the
#                       process: replica-set member state, oplog window, connections, asserts.
#
# THE SECOND GROUP IS NEW AND CLOSES THE GAP THE FIRST ONE COULD NOT. `node_systemd_unit_state`
# answers exactly one question -- has mongod exited -- and mongod's worst state does not involve
# exiting: a member that has stepped down, or that cannot see its own replica-set configuration,
# stays `active`, answers reads, and refuses every write. `MongodNotRunning` is silent throughout.
#
# WHAT IS WATCHED INSTEAD is the two things that can be seen from outside the process: whether the
# unit is running, and whether the backup that protects the data is still happening.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POST-MIGRATION AUDIT (2026-08-29): EVERY RULE HERE KEPT. AND IT NOW CARRIES A LOAD IT DID NOT.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `MongodNotRunning` is the REPLACEMENT for the Grafana rule "Mongo down"
# (`max(fly_instance_up{app="kinowo-mongo"})`), which is dropped where it lives in
# grafana/alerting/alert-rules.yaml. That rule was set to `noDataState: Alerting`, so with the Fly
# Mongo app stopped AND the Fly datasource's token revoked it had no way to do anything except fire
# permanently -- an always-on critical about a machine that no longer exists. This rule watches the
# real database on mongo-1 and is the only "is the database up" alert this fleet has.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT COVERED, so its absence does not read as an oversight:
#
#   * REPLICATION LAG, WHICH IS TRIVIALLY ZERO. `rs0` has one member (roles/mongodb.nix argues for
#     why: one member is enough for an oplog, and more members would be about availability this
#     fleet has not bought). A member cannot lag behind itself, so a lag rule here would be a
#     series pinned at 0 that can never fire -- coverage in the rule list and nothing in reality.
#     IT BECOMES REAL THE DAY A SECOND MEMBER IS ADDED, and that is the commit to write it in.
#   * QUERY LATENCY AND LOCK CONTENTION. The exporter can report both, but only through the
#     per-collection collectors that roles/mongodb-exporter.nix deliberately leaves off for
#     cardinality reasons, and nobody here has a threshold for them that is not invented. The
#     number a real incident bites at is the number to write here.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE METRIC NAMES BELOW WERE NOT READ OFF A RUNNING EXPORTER. CHECK THEM ONCE, ON FIRST DEPLOY.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# They are written against Percona's mongodb_exporter running with `--compatible-mode`, which is
# what roles/mongodb-exporter.nix passes and which is the flag that produces the older
# `mongodb_mongod_replset_*` family. They have not been verified against this fleet's own
# /metrics -- so the FIRST thing to do after the exporter is deployed is
#
#     curl -s 10.20.0.10:9216/metrics | grep -E 'replset_my_state|oplog_|ss_connections|ss_asserts'
#
# and correct anything that differs. A RULE AGAINST A SERIES THAT DOES NOT EXIST DOES NOT ERROR AND
# DOES NOT FIRE -- it sits in the rule list looking like coverage. That is the exact failure
# k3s.rules' header is written against, and the `absent()` companion beside each rule below is what
# turns it back into an alert: if a name here is wrong, the companion fires within half an hour and
# says so.
#
# EVERY RULE BELOW DEPENDS ON A node_exporter COLLECTOR BEING ENABLED, and this file cannot enforce
# that -- `node_systemd_unit_state` needs the systemd collector, and the two `kinowo_*` series are
# written into node_exporter's textfile directory by nix/modules/roles/mongodb.nix and
# nix/modules/roles/wireguard-fly.nix. A collector that is off does not produce an error; it
# produces silence, which is why each group carries an `absent()` companion.
groups:
  - name: mongodb
    rules:
      - alert: MongodNotRunning
        expr: node_systemd_unit_state{name="mongodb.service",state="active"} == 0
        # THREE MINUTES, NOT THIRTY SECONDS: an ordinary restart -- a config change, a deploy --
        # takes mongod through inactive on the way back up, and paging for every one of those
        # teaches everyone to ignore the alert that also fires when it does not come back.
        for: 3m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "mongod is not running on {{ $labels.host }}"
          description: >-
            The database is down. The web tier will keep serving its projected read model from
            memory and its health checks will stay green, so THE SITE WILL LOOK FINE while it stops
            updating and every write from the worker fails. Check `journalctl -u mongodb -n 200`;
            the two failures worth ruling out first are the data volume not being mounted (the unit
            has RequiresMountsFor, so it refuses to start rather than creating an empty database)
            and the replica-set key file's permissions, which mongod checks at startup.

      - alert: MongodUnitStateAbsent
        expr: absent(node_systemd_unit_state{name="mongodb.service"})
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting the state of mongod's unit"
          description: >-
            `MongodNotRunning` above is a comparison and cannot fire without this series, so the
            database is currently unwatched. Either node_exporter's systemd collector is off or the
            mongo host has dropped out of scrape entirely (check `TargetDown`). It does NOT mean
            mongod is running.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE BACKUP. Hetzner's server backups DO NOT COVER ATTACHED VOLUMES, so this timer is the
      # only logical copy of the database that exists -- see the header of roles/mongodb.nix. A
      # backup nobody checks is a backup that has already stopped.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MongodumpStale
        expr: time() - kinowo_mongodump_last_success_timestamp_seconds > 36 * 3600
        # NO `for:`. The expression already carries its own patience -- 36 hours against a nightly
        # timer is one full missed run plus most of a day's grace, so a slow dump or a reboot at
        # the wrong moment does not page.
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No successful mongodump for over 36 hours"
          description: >-
            The nightly dump has not completed since {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} ago. Check
            `systemctl status mongodump` and `journalctl -u mongodump`. Remember what this covers
            and what it does not: it is a logical copy on the SAME volume as the database, so it
            protects against a bad migration or a dropped collection and not against losing the
            volume. Off-box replication of these dumps is a known gap.

      - alert: MongodumpMetricAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_mongodump_last_success_timestamp_seconds)
        # SIX HOURS, comfortably longer than a boot and shorter than the gap between two dumps: the
        # series is written by the dump itself, so it is legitimately absent on a host that has
        # never run one -- which is exactly the state a fresh build is in, and exactly the state
        # somebody needs to be told about.
        for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is publishing mongodump's success timestamp"
          description: >-
            `MongodumpStale` cannot fire without this series, so the backup is unwatched. Either
            the dump has never run on this host, or node_exporter is not reading the textfile
            directory the dump writes to (`--collector.textfile.directory` must point at
            fleet.mongodb.backup.textfileDirectory). AN ABSENT SERIES IS NOT A HEALTHY BACKUP.

      - alert: MongodumpShrankSharply
        # Half the size of the smallest dump in the last week. A database that legitimately loses
        # half its content overnight is an incident in its own right; far more likely is a dump
        # that failed part-way and still wrote a directory, or one taken against the wrong
        # database. Either way it is the shape that makes a restore fail when it is needed.
        expr: kinowo_mongodump_last_size_bytes < 0.5 * min_over_time(kinowo_mongodump_last_size_bytes[7d])
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "The last mongodump is less than half the size of the smallest in the past week"
          description: >-
            A backup that is present but truncated is worse than a missing one, because it passes
            every check that only asks whether a dump exists. Compare the directory listings under
            fleet.mongodb.backup.directory before the next rotation removes the older copy.

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE EXPORTER'S GROUP. Everything here needs mongodb_exporter on mongo-1 to be running AND
  # authenticated; see this file's header for how the names were arrived at and why each rule has an
  # `absent()` companion.
  #
  # THE `host` AND `role` LABELS come from the `mongodb` scrape job in
  # /etc/prometheus/scrape.d/mongodb-targets.yaml, which roles/prometheus.nix generates from
  # `fleet.prometheus.mongodbExporterTargets` -- the same mechanism, and the same caveat, as the
  # `node` job's labels described in host-health.rules.
  #
  # NOTE WHAT IS NOT WRITTEN HERE: "the exporter is unreachable". `TargetDown` in
  # monitoring-self.rules already fires on `up == 0` for ANY job and names the job and instance, so
  # a second copy scoped to this one would page twice for one event. What IS written is the case
  # `TargetDown` cannot see -- an exporter that is answering perfectly well and cannot get an answer
  # out of mongod.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - name: mongodb-exporter
    rules:
      - alert: MongodExporterCannotReachMongod
        expr: mongodb_up == 0
        # THREE MINUTES, matching `MongodNotRunning`'s, and for the same reason: an ordinary mongod
        # restart takes the connection with it, and paging for every deploy teaches everyone to
        # ignore the alert that also fires when it does not come back.
        for: 3m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "mongodb_exporter cannot reach mongod on {{ $labels.host }}"
          description: >-
            The exporter is running and answering scrapes; the DATABASE is what it cannot talk to.
            THIS IS NOT THE SAME ALERT AS `MongodNotRunning` and the difference is the point: if
            that one is silent, mongod's unit is `active` and the failure is between the exporter
            and the server. Three things produce that, in the order worth checking -- the
            `kinowo_monitor` user does not exist or its password in nix/secrets/mongo-1.yaml
            (`mongodb/exporter-password`) does not match, mongod has been restarted into
            `fleet.mongodb.bootstrapMode` (auth off, loopback only), or mongod is alive but wedged
            and answering nothing. `journalctl -u mongodb-exporter -n 50` names which.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE HEADLINE RULE, AND THE REASON THE EXPORTER EXISTS AT ALL.
      #
      # Member states: 1 PRIMARY, 2 SECONDARY, 0 STARTUP, 3 RECOVERING, 5 STARTUP2, 6 UNKNOWN,
      # 8 DOWN, 9 ROLLBACK, 10 REMOVED.
      #
      # 1 OR 2, NOT 1 ALONE, AND THE SECOND VALUE WAS ADDED THE DAY THE SET STOPPED BEING ONE
      # MEMBER. This was `!= 1` while `rs0` had exactly one member, where the only healthy state is
      # PRIMARY. Adding a second member to move the database between Hetzner locations (see
      # ansible/playbooks/migrate-mongo-replica.yml) makes SECONDARY a permanently healthy state on
      # exactly one of the two hosts -- so the old expression would have fired `critical`, for ever,
      # against a member doing precisely what it was built to do, starting the moment Prometheus
      # first scraped it.
      #
      # Everything else is still covered by exclusion rather than by a list of bad states, so a new
      # state code from a future server version alerts by default -- which remains the safe
      # direction. What is NOT covered is "no member is primary": every member reporting 2 is two
      # healthy-looking secondaries and a database refusing writes. `MongodNoPrimary` below is that
      # rule, and it is the one that matters on a set with more than one member.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MongodNotPrimary
        expr: mongodb_mongod_replset_my_state != 1 and mongodb_mongod_replset_my_state != 2
        # TWO MINUTES, SHORTER THAN `MongodNotRunning`'s THREE, and deliberately so. A restart passes
        # through STARTUP2 and RECOVERING on the way to PRIMARY, so some patience is required -- but
        # every second spent here is a second of writes failing while the site looks healthy, which
        # is worse than a second of mongod being visibly down. Two minutes clears an ordinary
        # restart on this dataset and not much more.
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "mongod on {{ $labels.host }} is not PRIMARY (state {{ $value }})"
          description: >-
            THIS MEMBER IS NEITHER PRIMARY NOR A HEALTHY SECONDARY. State 0/3/5 is a member
            still starting or recovering and may clear on its own; 6, 8 or 10 usually means mongod
            cannot see the replica-set configuration it is registered in, which on this fleet means
            the member host in that config (`fleet.mongodb.replSetMemberHost`) is not an address
            mongod is bound to. `mongosh --eval 'rs.status()'` on the box is the first command.

            A SECONDARY (state 2) IS NOT THIS ALERT and never was intended to be -- see the note on
            the expression. The failure where the set is up and refusing every write is
            `MongodNoPrimary`: reads succeed, writes fail, and CHANGE STREAMS -- which the web
            tier's whole live-update path depends on -- cannot be opened, while the read tier keeps
            serving its projected read model out of memory and passing its health checks. NOTHING
            LOOKS BROKEN while the showtimes age.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE RULE THAT `MongodNotPrimary` STOPPED BEING when SECONDARY became healthy. With one
      # member, "not primary" and "no primary" were the same sentence. With two they are not: every
      # member can report a perfectly healthy 2 while the set has no leader at all, which is a
      # database that serves reads and refuses every write -- the exact failure the old wording
      # described, now needing its own expression.
      #
      # `absent()` IS NOT USABLE HERE. The series does not disappear when nobody is primary; it is
      # present and equal to 2 on every member. Counting is the only way to ask the question, and
      # `max` over the whole set is deliberate -- one primary anywhere is enough.
      #
      # AND THE `== 0` ON THE END IS THE WHOLE RULE. An alerting expression fires on the PRESENCE of
      # a sample, never on its truth, so `max(state == 1) or vector(0)` -- which is what this was --
      # returns a sample of 1 exactly when a member IS primary and paged continuously against a
      # healthy set, going quiet only in the situation it was written to catch. The comparison is
      # what turns the two branches into an answer: the left branch survives it only when it is
      # empty and `vector(0)` has stood in for it, which is to say when no member reports state 1.
      # Nothing reporting at all lands on the same branch and fires too, deliberately -- a monitor
      # that cannot see a primary should say so; `MongodReplsetStateAbsent` below explains why.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MongodNoPrimary
        expr: (max(mongodb_mongod_replset_my_state == 1) or vector(0)) == 0
        # THE SAME TWO MINUTES AS `MongodNotPrimary`, for the same reason: an election is seconds,
        # and a deliberate step-down during a migration should not page. Anything longer than a
        # couple of minutes without a leader is not an election any more.
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "no mongod in rs0 is PRIMARY"
          description: >-
            THE SET HAS NO LEADER AND IS REFUSING EVERY WRITE. Reads still succeed and change
            streams cannot be opened, so the read tier goes on serving its projection out of memory
            and passing health checks while the showtimes age -- nothing looks broken.

            On a set with one voting member this means that member is not PRIMARY and
            `MongodNotPrimary` will say which state it is in instead. On a set mid-migration it
            usually means a reconfig left two voters and one of them is unreachable, so neither can
            take a majority: `mongosh --eval 'rs.status()'` on either box names the member that is
            not answering.

      - alert: MongodReplsetStateAbsent
        expr: absent(mongodb_mongod_replset_my_state)
        # THIRTY MINUTES, matching every other `absent()` companion in this directory. A rule about
        # a rule does not need to be quick.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting mongod's replica-set member state"
          description: >-
            `MongodNotPrimary` above is a comparison and cannot fire without this series, so the
            single failure this exporter was deployed to catch is currently uncovered. THE MOST
            LIKELY CAUSE IS NOT AN OUTAGE, it is a name: this series exists only because
            roles/mongodb-exporter.nix passes `--compatible-mode`, and it is one of the names this
            file's header says to verify against a live /metrics on first deploy. Check the flag and
            the spelling before looking for a fault. If the exporter itself were down, `TargetDown`
            would have said so first.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE OPLOG WINDOW -- the change-stream resume budget, and the number roles/mongodb.nix pins
      # `oplogSizeMB` to protect.
      #
      # WHAT IT MEANS HERE, because "oplog" alone undersells it. A change-stream consumer that is
      # disconnected for longer than this window cannot resume from its stored token: it gets
      # ChangeStreamHistoryLost and falls back to a full re-read, which is this application's
      # expensive path and the one the resume token exists to avoid. So this is not a "database
      # health" number, it is HOW LONG A WORKER OUTAGE THIS FLEET SURVIVES GRACEFULLY.
      #
      # THE WINDOW IS TIME, NOT SIZE, and that is why it is worth alerting on at all. The oplog is
      # 1GB -- cut from 4GB when the volume was sized at Hetzner's 10GB minimum -- so the window is
      # whatever 1GB of writes happens to be, which shrinks the moment write volume rises. A size
      # alert would say nothing; a time alert says the thing that matters.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MongodOplogWindowShort
        expr: >-
          mongodb_mongod_replset_oplog_head_timestamp
            - mongodb_mongod_replset_oplog_tail_timestamp < 4 * 3600
        # FOUR HOURS IS A GUESS AND IT HAS NOT BEEN MEASURED. The reasoning: the window has to
        # comfortably outlast the longest planned interruption to a change-stream consumer, and the
        # longest one this fleet has is a worker redeploy or a mongo-1 reboot -- minutes. Four hours
        # is two orders of magnitude above that, which leaves room for an unplanned outage nobody is
        # awake for, and is low enough that on a 1GB oplog against a ~70MB database it should never
        # be approached in normal operation. IF IT FIRES IN THE FIRST MONTH, the write rate is
        # higher than that assumption and the number to compare against is
        # `db.getReplicationInfo().timeDiff` under normal load -- not this threshold lowered until
        # it stops firing.
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "The oplog window on {{ $labels.host }} is down to {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            This is the RESUME BUDGET for the application's change streams, not a disk warning.
            While it is this short, any consumer offline for longer than the window comes back to
            ChangeStreamHistoryLost and a full re-read. It shrinks for one of two reasons and they
            need opposite responses: a burst of writes (transient -- check whether the worker is
            backfilling), or a sustained rise in write rate (structural -- the oplog is now too
            small for the workload). THE FIX FOR THE SECOND IS NOT FREE: `oplogSizeMB` was cut from
            4096 to 1024 because the data volume is 5GB, so growing the window means growing the
            Hetzner volume first (it grows in place; see terraform/server.mongo.tf).

      - alert: MongodOplogWindowMetricAbsent
        expr: absent(mongodb_mongod_replset_oplog_head_timestamp)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting the oplog window on mongo-1"
          description: >-
            `MongodOplogWindowShort` is a comparison and cannot fire without this series. As with
            the replica-set state above, suspect the METRIC NAME first -- these are
            `--compatible-mode` names this file's header says to verify once against a live
            /metrics. The other candidate is the `kinowo_monitor` user having been created without
            `clusterMonitor`: the oplog is measured out of `local.oplog.rs`, and `clusterMonitor` is
            what grants read on `local`. A user with a narrower role authenticates and scrapes
            perfectly well, and simply publishes fewer series.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # CONNECTIONS. mongod's ceiling here is not a config value in roles/mongodb.nix -- it is
      # derived from `LimitNOFILE` (64000 on this unit), so the "available" figure is the honest
      # denominator and a hard-coded one would be wrong the first time that limit changes.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MongodConnectionsSaturated
        expr: >-
          sum by (host) (mongodb_ss_connections{conn_type="current"})
            / sum by (host) (mongodb_ss_connections{conn_type=~"current|available"}) > 0.8
        # 80% IS A GUESS, and a soft one. Reasoning: connection exhaustion is not gradual -- past
        # the ceiling mongod refuses new connections outright, and every client on this fleet
        # (the worker, the web tier, the dump timer) treats that as a hard failure. 80% is far
        # enough below to leave time to act and high enough that ordinary pool growth does not
        # reach it, since the pools involved are tens of connections against a ceiling in the tens
        # of thousands. IF THIS EVER FIRES it is far more likely to be a connection LEAK in a
        # client than legitimate load.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "mongod on {{ $labels.host }} is using {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its connection ceiling"
          description: >-
            The ceiling comes from `LimitNOFILE` on the mongodb unit (64000), not from a MongoDB
            setting, so raising it is a change to roles/mongodb.nix. Before doing that, find out who
            is holding them: `db.currentOp(true)` and `db.serverStatus().connections` name the
            clients. A pool that grows without bound is a client bug, and raising the ceiling for it
            postpones the same failure at greater cost.

      - alert: MongodAssertionsRising
        # `rollovers` EXCLUDED DELIBERATELY: it counts the assert counters wrapping, not a fault.
        expr: sum by (host) (rate(mongodb_ss_asserts{assert_type!="rollovers"}[15m])) > 1
        # ONE PER SECOND, SUSTAINED FOR FIFTEEN MINUTES, IS A GUESS -- and, like `MemoryStallHigh` in
        # host-health.rules, it is a floor above the noise rather than a tuned number. A healthy
        # mongod sits at approximately zero here; `user` asserts are individual malformed or
        # unauthorised operations and a handful is ordinary, so the threshold is set where "a
        # handful" becomes "something is doing this constantly".
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "mongod on {{ $labels.host }} is raising {{ $value }} assertions/s"
          description: >-
            ASSERTIONS ARE A LEADING INDICATOR, NOT AN OUTAGE, which is why this is a warning. Check
            `assert_type` on the series before deciding anything: `user` means clients are sending
            operations mongod rejects (a bad query, a failed authentication -- often a deploy that
            changed a credential), `warning` and `msg` are internal and are worth reading
            /var/log/mongodb/mongod.log for, and a sustained `regular` count is the one that
            suggests the server itself is unhappy.

      - alert: MongodServerStatusSeriesAbsent
        expr: absent(mongodb_ss_connections)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting mongod's serverStatus counters"
          description: >-
            `MongodConnectionsSaturated` and `MongodAssertionsRising` are both comparisons against
            this collector's output and neither can fire without it. This one series stands in for
            the whole `diagnosticdata` collector: if it is absent, `--collector.diagnosticdata` has
            been dropped from the exporter's ExecStart, the metric names have moved, or the
            `kinowo_monitor` user lost the role that lets it run `serverStatus`. IT DOES NOT MEAN
            THE DATABASE IS HEALTHY.
